<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" id="fiberopticghostface" class="jumpanchor aligncenter size-full wp-image-3616" src="https://www.ghostface.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/fiberopticghostfacebannerwp.png" alt="Fiber Optic GhostFace Mask" width="650" height="112" srcset="https://www.ghostface.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/fiberopticghostfacebannerwp.png 650w, https://www.ghostface.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/fiberopticghostfacebannerwp-300x52.png 300w, https://www.ghostface.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/fiberopticghostfacebannerwp-24x4.png 24w, https://www.ghostface.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/fiberopticghostfacebannerwp-36x6.png 36w, https://www.ghostface.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/fiberopticghostfacebannerwp-48x8.png 48w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 650px) 100vw, 650px" /><br />
Here we have another version which looks to be &#8216;based off&#8217; the Bleeding Version molded mask, in this case we have of course, no blood, but instead, small Fiber optic lights that highlight the main face features, such as the eyes, the nose, and the mouth, on pressing the button the lights would continually flash whilst pressed, or if you simply used the switch on the box and turned it on then then the lights would continue until switched off again.</p>
<p>To my knowledge and after looking online, i see there is limited info on this type, but i can easily see it had two different types of packaging in its time, one similar to the Bleeding Mask packaging when it first arrived, and another simply being a thick cardboard header tag (Same cardboard thickness seen on the later 2010 Scarecrow Scream Header tag), and a tagged black box on the bottom of the hood with the usual &#8216;Try me&#8217;  (On packaged version), and &#8216;Battery Housing in your pocket&#8217; in the Yellow star&#8217; as seen on the &#8216;Black and Red&#8217; style Mask header tags.</p>
<p>It is &#8216;Assumed&#8217; that these were either late 90&#8217;s produced after the Bleeding mask was more known, or possibly not too long after 2000. The reason why there are two types of packaging, and the fact there were &#8216;Tagged&#8217; types out there is currently unknown. Still an interesting mask though, and also one fitting the &#8216;Hen&#8217;s Teeth&#8217; of Rarity category! More so with the Tagged type.</p>
